1
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,111
An Extreme puzzle begins simply enough.

2
00:00:08,391 --> 00:00:12,357
A few cross-hatching moves reveal a confined group: one and seven.

3
00:00:12,937 --> 00:00:14,376
Then comes the fork.

4
00:00:14,696 --> 00:00:16,577
Two positions remain for two.

5
00:00:16,857 --> 00:00:21,592
We choose one—not because we know it's right, but because we can follow where it leads.

6
00:00:22,172 --> 00:00:24,186
One placement forces another.

7
00:00:24,386 --> 00:00:25,951
Box six resolves.

8
00:00:26,271 --> 00:00:28,174
Still, no conflict.

9
00:00:29,094 --> 00:00:30,437
The branch holds.

10
00:00:31,517 --> 00:00:34,299
Now two boxes constrain seven in parallel.

11
00:00:34,549 --> 00:00:37,876
That fixes another seven, and the puzzle begins to open.

12
00:00:38,496 --> 00:00:40,526
Soon, every cell is filled.

13
00:00:41,476 --> 00:00:43,442
But completion isn't proof.

14
00:00:44,222 --> 00:00:45,948
Return to the assumption.

15
00:00:46,198 --> 00:00:48,096
Trace it through the finished grid.

16
00:00:48,346 --> 00:00:49,656
No duplicate.

17
00:00:49,956 --> 00:00:51,522
No contradiction.

18
00:00:52,442 --> 00:00:53,625
Verified.

19
00:00:54,575 --> 00:00:56,734
Sometimes the branch doesn't break.

20
00:00:57,254 --> 00:00:59,108
It leads all the way home.
